## P0-5 — `run_id` cannot perform the pairing SR-18 assigns to it

### Evidence

`params.artifacts.run_id_key` contains `system`. Therefore learned and classical runs at the same dataset/SNR/seed necessarily have different IDs. SR-18 nevertheless says ER-10 pairs systems “entirely through `run_id`”.

The key also omits `split`, checkpoint/config identity, classifier variant, λ, control dimension/quantiser choice, and other result-changing fields. The per-image schema carries none of the missing pairing coordinates.

The current collision test only rejects one ID mapping to different tuples. It does not prove that:

- each full tuple gets a unique ID;
- validation and test cannot collide;
- two systems share the same image/noise draw;
- reruns with different checkpoints cannot overwrite or silently join.

### Required correction

Separate identity from pairing:

- `run_id`: a unique content-addressed ID for one system run, including system, dataset/version, split manifest, code/config hash, checkpoint hash, classifier variant, all seeds, ratio, SNR, and system-specific settings.
- `noise_id`: a deterministic ID keyed by dataset manifest, split, stable sample ID, SNR, channel seed, channel model, channel-use budget, block index, and RNG purpose.
- `analysis_cell_id`: the preregistered compound replicate, including the learned/control training seed and its paired channel seed.
- `pair_id`: a system- and comparison-independent key built from analysis cell, stable sample ID, ratio, SNR, and `noise_id`. Analysis metadata names the two systems being compared, so one learned per-image row can participate in both learned-versus-classical and learned-versus-ER9 analyses.

The per-image schema should carry at least `run_id`, `pair_id`, `noise_id`, dataset version, split, stable sample ID, ratio, SNR, analysis cell, true/predicted label, delivery/outage fields, and source/payload accounting.

Use counter-based or keyed RNG streams. A common integer seed with conditionally consumed random numbers does not guarantee common noise or fallback labels across implementations with different control flow.

### Exit test

Tests must prove:

- row reordering and batching do not change any `noise_id` or draw;
- systems share the required `pair_id` and noise but have distinct `run_id`s;
- split/checkpoint/config changes always change `run_id`;
- system changes never change `pair_id` when the comparison cell is otherwise identical;
- joins are one-to-one and reject missing/duplicate trajectories.

## P0-6 — AM-49’s packetisation evidence is materially wrong

### Evidence

[`spec/evidence/check_packetisation.py`](../spec/evidence/check_packetisation.py) returns zero failures, but direct checks of its 215 feasible rows find:

| Defect | Affected rows | Currently “live” rows |
|---|---:|---:|
| `A` is not byte-aligned despite `payload_solver: largest_byte_aligned_A...` | 92/215 | 29/72 |
| \(B'/C\) is nonintegral but `segment()` silently applies `ceil` | 21/215 | 16/72 |
| Union of the preceding two | 97/215 | 30/72 |
| BG2 filler uses selection \(K_b\) instead of encoded \(K=10Z\) | 47/103 BG2 rows | 0/14 current-live BG2 rows |

TS 38.212 §5.2.2 defines \(K'=B'/C\). The project supplies arbitrary source sizes rather than a TS 38.214 transport-block-size table, so the local solver must choose legal byte-framed values for which the division is integral, or explicitly disclose and account for a non-NR outer padding rule. It currently does neither.

For BG2, \(K_b=6,8,9,10\) is used to select a lifting size for short blocks, but the encoded systematic length is still \(K=10Z\). The script computes filler as \(K_bZ-K'\), undercounting 47 rows. It also emits one scalar that is a per-code-block filler count while the schema and prose read like a transport-block total. In 62 segmented configurations the total must include every code block.

The canonical worked case changes:

| Field | Current AM-49 claim | Correct byte/divisibility-constrained result |
|---|---:|---:|
| Payload \(A\) | 42,642 bits | 42,624 bits |
| Source bytes | 5,330 | 5,328 |
| \(B\) after TB CRC | 42,666 | 42,648 |
| Code blocks \(C\) | 6 | 6 |
| \(B'\) | 42,810 | 42,792 |
| \(K'\) per block | 7,135 | 7,132 |
| Lifting size \(Z\) | 352 | 352 |
| Filler | “609 bits” | 612 per block; 3,672 total |
| \(E_r\) | sums to 51,200 | `[8532, 8532, 8534, 8534, 8534, 8534]`, sum 51,200 |

The good news is important: a corrected solver still leaves **215 feasible configurations and all 72 currently designated live configurations feasible**. It changes source-byte capacity in only 18 rows (13 current-live), by −8 to +1 byte. This is a repair, not a scientific collapse.

Additional evidence defects:

- `live_ratios` is built from three provisional parameter values. ER-3 may select any ladder ratio; proof obligations must cover all headline-dataset candidates, not only today’s provisional three.
- ER-9 feasibility checks only `dimension × bits <= A` and claims range coding can only shrink. Finite arithmetic/range streams and framing can expand.
- the strict rate-floor comparison creates three apparent STL clamps at equality that the library accepts; with the corrected predicate the reported six clamps reduce to the three Imagenette cases, so AM-24’s “same six clamps” conclusion must be amended. The floor must be checked for every block using \(K'/E_r\), not only against `E[0]`.
- the checked-in packet record lacks a source config/params hash.

The controlling primary source is the pinned [3GPP TS 38.212 V17.13.0 archive](https://www.3gpp.org/ftp/specs/archive/38_series/38.212/38212-hd0.zip), especially §§5.2.2, 5.4.2, and 7.2. Archive its checksum with the standards register rather than depending only on a mutable Release-17 directory.

### Required correction

The solver must:

- enumerate byte-aligned \(A\);
- require \(B'\bmod C=0\), unless a separately named and counted outer pad is deliberately adopted;
- make the optimizer unique: first choose the largest legal \(A\) satisfying \(A+L_{\text{TB}}(A)\le\lfloor G R_{\text{nominal}}\rfloor\); only if that candidate violates the library’s base-graph rate floor, apply a narrowly named clamp that chooses the **smallest** larger legal \(A\) satisfying every per-block floor, and record nominal versus realized rate;
- record \(B'\), \(K'\), \(K\), \(Z\), every \(E_r\), source padding, segmentation padding, filler per block, and total filler;
- use \(K=22Z\) for BG1 and \(K=10Z\) for BG2;
- calculate and test the exact library minimum-rate convention for every block—equivalently use the largest \(E_r\) when checking the minimum \(K'/E_r\)—with equality accepted;
- validate every candidate ratio for Imagenette and STL-10;
- give ER-9 a counted selector bit and `min(range-coded, fixed-width raw)` escape, or reserve a proven worst-case framing expansion;
- hash the exact generated params and evidence script into the record.

### Exit test

Assert over the whole grid:

```text
A % 8 == 0
B_prime % C == 0
C * K_prime == B_prime
K == 22*Z for BG1
K == 10*Z for BG2
filler_total == sum(K - K_prime for each block)
sum(E_r) == G
every E_r % Q_m == 0
complete source/framing/padding accounting == A
K_prime/E_r >= configured_floor(base_graph) for every block
A is maximal under the nominal-rate predicate, or minimal under the named floor override
```

Add independent boundary vectors around A=292, A=3824, both `K_cb` values, base-graph switches, lifting-size switches, segmentation-count changes, and \(E_r\) distribution changes. Regenerate the JSON and revise AM-49’s canonical numbers, evidence README, crossover explanation, and every dependent runtime/payload statement.

## P0-7 — The PHY seam remains underspecified in ways that can silently weaken the baseline

### Omitted modulation bit interleaver

TS 38.212 §5.4.2.2’s modulation bit interleaver must be explicit for QPSK/16-QAM. Sionna applies the output interleaver when `num_bits_per_symbol=Q_m` is given to the encoder; the W0 encoder probes instantiate without it. Omitting it changes how unequal 16-QAM bit channels receive coded bits and can silently reduce the baseline.

Add a named parameter, pass \(Q_m\) at the seam, invert consistently at the decoder, and validate QPSK/16-QAM rate-matched vectors.

### CRC and decoder details

`cb_crc_bits: 24` must name CRC24B. Pin CRC16, CRC24A, and CRC24B polynomials, bit order, initialization, final XOR, and known-answer vectors. G-2 says the offset-min-sum offset is pinned, but no parameter currently pins it; Sionna’s documented default is 0.5. Pin and assert it rather than inheriting a library default. Also pin clipping, iteration schedule, and any LLR saturation.

TS 38.212 does not provide the published BER/BLER curve G-2 asks to match. Name a real, downloadable reference dataset/config/checksum now. The reference must match \((K,N)\), base graph, lifting size, modulation, decoder algorithm, offset, iterations, and SNR convention.

### Mapping, demapping, and energy

Pin TS 38.211 Gray mapping/scaling for QPSK and 16-QAM, the demapper formula (APP or max-log), noise-variance convention, and the already discovered `log(p1/p0)` sign at one tested seam.

The current “equal average symbol power equals equal transmitted energy” wording is exact for the learned per-image normalization and constant-modulus BPSK/QPSK. A normalized 16-QAM constellation has average energy 1 **under uniform labels**, but the project has not specified a post-rate-matching scrambler that guarantees uniform magnitude-bit labels, and finite packets fluctuate in either case. Per-packet rescaling would create a data-dependent gain and require the receiver to know/count it.

Preferred policy: use one fixed normalized constellation, do not secretly rescale each codeword, and log realized packet/dataset energy. Either add a deterministic receiver-known whitening/scrambling rule before mapping or report any deviation from uniform-label constellation energy. State fairness as equal channel uses and measured aggregate/expected symbol energy for the digital arm. If exact per-packet equality is retained, specify how the gain reaches the receiver and count or symmetrically exclude it.

### Standards claim scope

The design uses the NR LDPC/CRC/segmentation/rate-matching core over an abstract AWGN channel. It does not implement TS 38.214 TBS/MCS/resource allocation, OFDM/resource mapping, HARQ, or a full NR link; plain BPSK is not NR uplink π/2-BPSK. Label it accurately:

> “A TS 38.212-derived 5G NR LDPC coding and rate-matching core, with custom byte-framed payloads and generic BPSK/QPSK/16-QAM over abstract AWGN.”

Do not call it complete NR packetisation/link conformance unless the missing standards layers are implemented.

The relevant library behavior is documented in [Sionna’s LDPC encoder source](https://nvlabs.github.io/sionna/_modules/sionna/phy/fec/ldpc/encoding.html), including `num_bits_per_symbol`. Because that documentation URL can track the current release, archive the exact Sionna 2.0.1 wheel/source hash and the relevant source excerpt in the evidence record rather than inferring behavior from the W0 probe or a mutable webpage.

### Exit test

G-2 must cover mapper/demapper/bit-interleaver known answers for \(Q_m=1,2,4\), all three CRCs, both base graphs, segmentation and rate matching, the LLR sign, decoder offset, and the named BLER reference. A sign-flipped or bit-interleaver-disabled 16-QAM fixture must fail loudly.

## P0-8 — W1’s data/config/environment contract is not frozen enough

### Dataset and split provenance

The params give counts and a seed but not:

- exact source/version URL and archive SHA-256;
- licence and class-to-index mapping;
- stable sample-ID rule;
- whether the validation carve is stratified;
- pre-shuffle ordering and exact RNG algorithm;
- committed split-manifest path/hash.

A seed is not a portable split specification when loader ordering and library behavior can change. Commit a manifest of stable IDs and labels, hash it, and test train/validation/test disjointness and counts.

Current torchvision documentation includes an official [`torchvision.datasets.Imagenette`](https://docs.pytorch.org/vision/master/generated/torchvision.datasets.Imagenette.html) loader. The implementation should either pin and use that exact loader/version/integrity behavior or pin a direct archive/checksum; it should not rely on the stale nonnormative assumption that Imagenette is absent from torchvision.

### Preprocessing

SR-19 exists and correctly requires common canonical pixels, but its parameters do not fix:

- random-resized-crop scale/ratio;
- horizontal-flip probability;
- model channel mean/std;
- whether canonical resize/crop and training augmentation are one transform or two;
- codec downsample/receiver-upsample interpolation and crop behavior.

Freeze one canonical evaluation image and one training augmentation pipeline. Test that the codec and learned encoder receive byte-identical canonical pixels before their system-specific operations.

Add SR-19 explicitly to the W1 schedule row and to G-1’s acceptance criteria. Its existence elsewhere in the spec does not currently stop W1 data from flowing before the canonical-pixel contract is accepted.

### G-1

G-1 must be validation-only and should gate the durable W1 outputs, not only an accuracy scalar:

- dataset download/integrity and all manifests;
- registry coverage for all three datasets;
- config round-trip and no experiment constants hidden in source;
- canonical-pixel identity;
- dependency/accelerator smoke;
- classifier log/checkpoint/config provenance;
- exact approved fallback ladder.

“Switch backbone or extend training” is too open. It permits architecture and compute changes after inspecting a gate metric. Name the order, maximum epochs, candidate backbones, selection split, parameter-count consequence, and stopping rule now.

### Environment

[`requirements.txt`](../requirements.txt) currently contains only `PyYAML>=6.0`. W0’s useful scratch environment is outside the repository and its install procedure is not a W1 lock.

Freeze:

- Python version;
- lock format and hashes;
- Torch/torchvision/Sionna/Glymur/OpenJPEG/scikit-image/analysis/demo versions;
- the CUDA wheel index and a `torch.version.cuda is not None` assertion;
- driver/device information captured in run metadata;
- torchvision op smoke tests;
- OpenJPEG runtime/version checks;
- a CPU installation path for analysis/demo where appropriate.

### Exit test

From a clean checkout, one documented command must create the environment, verify hashes, materialize manifests, run a data integrity/split/preprocessing test, train or resume the W1 classifier, and reproduce its validation metric and metadata schema without reading test samples. Pin deterministic backend settings and a numerical reproduction tolerance; a reasonable starting requirement is exact hashes/predictions for deterministic CPU fixtures and validation top-1 within 0.1 percentage point for a same-seed GPU retrain, while never falling below the G-1 floor.

